{"cause":"webkit-intrinsic-text-sizing","summary":"The WebKit paragraph is narrower and therefore centered farther right because its auto/intrinsic text sizing resolves to a smaller width than Chromium and Firefox.","mechanism":"The shell is the same 1120px wide in all engines, while the paragraph's resolved width, max-width, and auto-centering margin differ only in WebKit. Its font size, weight, padding, display, and wrapping behavior are otherwise the same. This indicates that the paragraph's unconstrained/intrinsic text width is participating in sizing, and WebKit's system-ui text metrics produce a 665.95px result instead of Chromium's roughly 691.78px and Firefox's 692.13px. The different width moves the element itself; the parent is not moving.","verdict":"defect","confidence":"medium","oddEngineOut":"webkit","fix":{"description":"Avoid relying on engine-dependent intrinsic sizing for the lede. Give it an explicit responsive width or max-width, then center it, preserving the intended 692px result at this viewport.","css":".lede {
  width: min(100%, 691.78px);
  margin-inline: auto;
}"},"evidenceCited":["ancestors.1","box.chromium","box.firefox","box.webkit","computedStylesSame.fontSize","computedStylesSame.fontWeight","computedStylesSame.paddingLeft","computedStylesSame.display","computedStyles.chromium.width","computedStyles.firefox.width","computedStyles.webkit.width","computedStyles.chromium.marginLeft","computedStyles.firefox.marginLeft","computedStyles.webkit.marginLeft"],"unresolved":["The collected styles do not include the authored width/max-width declarations or the text-align/inline-margin declarations, so the exact intrinsic-sizing rule cannot be named more specifically (for example, fit-content versus another auto-sized rule)."]}

{"cause":"webkit-content-sized-lede-width","summary":"WebKit gives the centered `.lede` paragraph a 665.95px used width rather than the roughly 692px maximum used by Chromium and Firefox, moving it 13px to the right.","mechanism":"This is a real used-width difference on the paragraph, not merely a positional difference: its computed `width` and `marginLeft` differ, while the `.shell` ancestor remains 1120px wide in every engine. The margins follow centering exactly: within the shell's 24px left padding, Chromium/Firefox's approximately 692px width leaves approximately 190px on each side, whereas WebKit's 665.95px width leaves 203.02px. The common 691.784px `maxWidth` is an upper bound, which Chromium and Firefox reach but WebKit does not. The evidence therefore supports an engine difference in the paragraph's content-dependent sizing/shrink-to-fit calculation before auto-margin centering. It does not establish which uncollected author `width`/margin rule creates that content-dependent sizing, nor a font-specific cause.","verdict":"expected-engine-difference","confidence":"medium","oddEngineOut":"webkit","fix":{"description":"Avoid relying on content-dependent used width for this prose block if identical geometry is required. Give the lede a definite responsive width and center it, while retaining the existing cap.","css":".lede {
  width: min(100%, 691.784px);
  margin-inline: auto;
}"},"evidenceCited":["finding.what differs.width","finding.what differs.offset-x","finding.computed styles that differ.width","finding.computed styles that differ.marginLeft","finding.computed styles, same in all three.maxWidth","finding.ancestors[0]","tools.read_computed_styles(BODY[1]/MAIN[1]/SECTION[1]/DIV[1]/P[1])","tools.read_computed_styles(BODY[1]/MAIN[1]/SECTION[1]/DIV[1])"],"unresolved":["The collected styles do not include the author-specified width value, right margin, or text alignment, so the precise sizing declaration cannot be identified.","No evidence establishes whether font shaping, intrinsic-size algorithm details, or another uncollected sizing input makes WebKit stop below the shared maximum."]}

{"cause":"engine-dependent-max-width-resolution","summary":"WebKit resolves the paragraph’s effective `max-width` to 609.24px rather than the roughly 633.8px used by Chromium and Firefox; the block therefore becomes 24.61px narrower and its automatic centering margins move it 12.30px right.","mechanism":"The width discrepancy is not caused by the parent or by wrapping: `.shell` is 1120px wide in every engine, while the paragraph’s used width matches its computed `maxWidth` in each engine. Chromium and Firefox resolve that limit to about 633.8px, but WebKit resolves it to 609.24px. Since the paragraph is a static block with no horizontal padding or border, its width is capped directly by that engine-specific maximum. Its left margin then increases from about 219.1px to 231.4px, exactly half of the 24.61px width reduction, which is the signature of horizontal auto-centering. The evidence establishes divergent resolution of the max-width expression, but the collected computed style serializes it only as pixels, so it cannot establish the authoring unit or the lower-level reason WebKit resolved it differently.","verdict":"defect","confidence":"high","oddEngineOut":"webkit","fix":{"description":"Avoid an engine-dependent maximum-width expression for this text measure, or provide a fixed pixel/rem cap if identical geometry is required. The centering itself does not need changing.","css":"/* Example: use a cross-engine fixed cap rather than a font-metric-dependent cap */
.head .shell > p {
  max-width: 633.84px;
  margin-inline: auto;
}"},"evidenceCited":["computed styles that differ.maxWidth","computed styles that differ.width","computed styles that differ.marginLeft","ancestors.1 <div> .shell","functions.read_computed_styles(BODY[1]/MAIN[1]/SECTION[1]/DIV[1]/P[1])","functions.read_computed_styles(BODY[1]/MAIN[1]/SECTION[1]/DIV[1])"],"unresolved":["The collected styles expose only resolved pixel values, not the authored max-width declaration. Therefore the evidence cannot determine whether the differing max-width came from ch/ex/font-relative sizing, another engine-sensitive CSS expression, or a page-specific engine-targeted rule."]}

